(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-female-reproductive-quiz"]={"title":"Female Reproductive System — Anatomy Exam 3","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The internal female reproductive organs develop primarily from which paired embryonic ducts?","choices":["Müllerian (paramesonephric) ducts","Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts","Pronephric embryonic ducts","Metanephric collecting ducts"],"answer":0,"exp":"The Müllerian (paramesonephric) ducts develop into the major internal female reproductive organs in the absence of anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH). — Slide 16 — Müllerian ducts (paramesonephric ducts)"},{"q":"The Müllerian ducts develop into the female organs in the absence of which hormone?","choices":["Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)","Anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH)","Progesterone","Estrogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"In the absence of anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH), the Müllerian ducts develop into the female reproductive organs; AMH causes their regression in males. — Slide 16–17 — regression in males occurs via AMH"},{"q":"The fused caudal portions of the Müllerian ducts (the uterovaginal primordium) give rise to which structures?","choices":["The fallopian tubes and fimbriae","The ovaries and ovarian ligaments","The uterus, cervix, and upper vagina","The labia majora and mons pubis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fused caudal portions form the uterus, cervix, and the upper portion of the vagina. — Slide 16 — fused caudal portions → uterus and cervix; upper vagina"},{"q":"The unfused cranial portions of the Müllerian ducts differentiate into which structures?","choices":["Uterus, cervix, and fundus","Clitoral glans and hood","Lower one-third of vagina","Fallopian (uterine) tubes"],"answer":3,"exp":"The unfused cranial portions become the fallopian (uterine) tubes. — Slide 16 — unfused cranial portions → Fallopian tubes"},{"q":"According to the lecture, the lower portion of the vagina develops from which structure?","choices":["The urogenital sinus","The fused Müllerian ducts","The mesonephric ducts","The coelomic epithelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lower vagina develops from the urogenital sinus, while the upper portion develops from the fused Müllerian ducts. — Slide 16 — lower vagina develops from the urogenital sinus"},{"q":"The perineum is a diamond-shaped area circumscribed anteriorly by which structure?","choices":["Coccyx","Pubic symphysis","Ischial tuberosities","Sacrum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The perineum is circumscribed anteriorly by the pubic symphysis (laterally by the ischial tuberosities and posteriorly by the coccyx). — Slide 5 — Perineum boundaries"},{"q":"The perineum is divided into two triangles by an imaginary horizontal line extending between which landmarks?","choices":["The pubic tubercles","The anterior superior iliac spines","The ischial tuberosities","The greater trochanters"],"answer":2,"exp":"An imaginary horizontal line between the ischial tuberosities of the ossa coxae divides the perineum into anterior and posterior triangles. — Slide 5 — line between the ischial tuberosities"},{"q":"The anterior triangle of the perineum is also called the urogenital triangle. In females it contains which openings?","choices":["The anus","The internal and external os","The base of the penis and scrotum","The urethral and vaginal orifices"],"answer":3,"exp":"The anterior (urogenital) triangle contains the urethral and vaginal orifices in females. — Slide 5 — Anterior (urogenital) triangle"},{"q":"The posterior triangle of the perineum, containing the anus in both sexes, is also called the:","choices":["Anal triangle","Urogenital triangle","Femoral triangle","Cystic triangle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The posterior triangle is the anal triangle, the location of the anus in both sexes. — Slide 5 — Posterior (anal) triangle"},{"q":"The pelvic diaphragm is formed by which muscles?","choices":["Bulbospongiosus and ischiocavernosus muscles","Levator ani and coccygeus muscles","Rectus abdominis and pyramidalis muscles","Piriformis and obturator internus muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm is formed by the levator ani and coccygeus muscles. — Slide 9 — Pelvic Diaphragm: levator ani and coccygeus"},{"q":"The levator ani muscle is made up of which three parts?","choices":["Internal, external, and mid sphincters","Superficial, middle, deep transverse perineal","Puborectalis, pubococcygeus, iliococcygeus","Ischiocavernosus, bulbospongiosus, coccygeus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The levator ani is made up of the puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and iliococcygeus. — Slide 9 — Levator ani: puborectalis, pubococcygeus, iliococcygeus"},{"q":"Which of the following is a function of the pelvic diaphragm described in the lecture?","choices":["Produces female gametes through monthly follicular maturation","Serves as the epithelial lining of the birth canal wall","Secretes reproductive hormones directly into systemic circulation","Supports pelvic organs and seals the inferior opening of the bony pelvis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm supports the pelvic organs, seals the inferior opening of the bony pelvis, and lifts to help release feces during defecation. — Slide 9 — Pelvic Diaphragm functions"},{"q":"Which muscle related to the vagina is described as part of the levator ani?","choices":["Pubovaginalis muscle","Bulbospongiosus muscle","External urethral sphincter","Urethrovaginal sphincter"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pubovaginalis muscle is described as part of the levator ani. — Slide 62 — Muscles Related to the Vagina"},{"q":"The broad ligament is best described as which of the following?","choices":["A double fold of peritoneum attaching the ovary to the pelvic wall","A fold of peritoneum that drapes over the uterus","A cord extending through the inguinal canal","A thickening of subserous fascia and smooth muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The broad ligament is a fold of peritoneum that drapes over the uterus (and helps hold the ovaries and uterine tubes). — Slide 14 — Broad ligament: peritoneum, drapes over the uterus"},{"q":"The mesovarium is a double fold of peritoneum that attaches the ovary to which structure?","choices":["The lateral pelvic wall","The uterine fundus","The broad ligament","The labia majora fold"],"answer":2,"exp":"The mesovarium is a double fold of peritoneum attaching the ovaries to the broad ligament. — Slide 14 — Mesovarium attaches ovaries to broad ligament"},{"q":"The ovarian ligament connects the ovary to which structure?","choices":["The pelvic wall","The labia majora","The broad ligament","The uterus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ovarian ligament runs from the ovary to the uterus (attaching to the superior margin of the uterus). — Slide 14, 18 — Ovarian ligament: ovary to uterus"},{"q":"The suspensory ligament of the ovary extends from the ovary/mesovarium to which structure?","choices":["The pelvic (body) wall","The uterine fundus wall","The cervical canal","The labia minora fold"],"answer":0,"exp":"The suspensory ligament extends from the ovary (mesovarium) to the pelvic/body wall and transmits the ovarian vessels. — Slide 14, 18 — Suspensory ligament: ovary to pelvic wall / body wall"},{"q":"The round ligament of the uterus extends from the uterus through the inguinal canal to which structure?","choices":["The lateral wall","The labia majora","The sacral base","The ovarian hilum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The round ligament extends from the uterus through the inguinal canal to the labia majora, helping maintain the anteverted position. — Slide 18, 36 — Round ligament to labia majora"},{"q":"The uterosacral ligaments extend from the inferior uterus to which structure?","choices":["The labia majora","The pubic symphysis","The sacrum","The lateral pelvic wall at the cervix"],"answer":2,"exp":"The uterosacral ligaments extend from the inferior uterus to the sacrum. — Slide 36 — Uterosacral ligaments: inferior uterus to sacrum"},{"q":"What are the primary sex organs (gonads) of the female?","choices":["The uterine tubes","The mammary glands","The uterus","The ovaries"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ovaries are the primary sex organs of the female; the uterine tubes, uterus, vagina, clitoris, and mammary glands are accessory sex organs. — Slide 12 — Primary sex organs are the ovaries"},{"q":"The ovary lies posterior to which peritoneal structure?","choices":["The broad ligament (mesovarium)","The round ligament of the uterus","The rectal peritoneal fold","The urinary bladder dome"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ovary lies posterior to the broad ligament (mesovarium) and is anchored by the suspensory and ovarian ligaments. — Slide 24 — Ovary posterior to broad ligament (mesovarium)"},{"q":"The ovary receives its arterial blood supply from which vessel?","choices":["The uterine artery","The ovarian artery","The internal iliac artery","The vaginal artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ovary's blood supply is via the ovarian artery, which arises from the abdominal aorta and enters through the suspensory ligament. — Slide 24, 27 — Blood supply via ovarian artery"},{"q":"The venous drainage of the right ovary is to the inferior vena cava, while the left ovarian vein drains into which vessel?","choices":["The left internal iliac vein","The portal vein","The left renal vein","The azygos vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right ovarian vein drains to the inferior vena cava and the left drains into the left renal vein. — Slide 24 — Venous drainage to IVC (right) and left renal vein"},{"q":"The lymphatics of the ovary follow the ovarian blood vessels to ascend to which nodes?","choices":["Superficial inguinal (groin) lymph nodes","Axillary (lateral breast) lymph nodes","Internal iliac (hypogastric) lymph nodes","Lumbar (para-aortic) lymph nodes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ovarian lymphatics follow the ovarian vessels to ascend to the lumbar lymph nodes. — Slide 27 — Lymphatics ascend to the lumbar lymph nodes"},{"q":"Afferent (sensory) fibers from the ovary enter the spinal cord through which nerve levels?","choices":["T10 and T11","L1 and L2","S2, S3, and S4","T6 and T7"],"answer":0,"exp":"Afferent fibers from the ovary enter the spinal cord through the T10 and T11 nerves. — Slide 27 — Afferent fibers enter spinal cord through T10 and T11"},{"q":"The uterus is located between which two organs?","choices":["The ovaries and the vagina","The urinary bladder and the rectum","The sacrum and the pubic symphysis","The ureters and the colon"],"answer":1,"exp":"The uterus lies between the urinary bladder (anteriorly) and the rectum (posteriorly). — Slide 32 — located between the urinary bladder and rectum"},{"q":"The normal position of the uterus is described as anteverted and anteflexed. Anteversion occurs at which junction?","choices":["The junction of internal os and isthmus","The junction of cervix and body","The junction of cervix and vagina","The fundus of the uterine body"],"answer":2,"exp":"The uterus is anteverted at the junction of the cervix and vagina (and anteflexed at the cervix and body). — Slide 31 — Anteverted at junction of cervix and vagina"},{"q":"Which is the rounded region of the uterus, superior and anterior to the entrance of the uterine tubes?","choices":["Body","Cervix","Isthmus","Fundus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The fundus is the rounded portion superior and anterior to the uterine tube entrance. — Slide 34 — Fundus"},{"q":"The constricted region of the uterus between the body and the cervix is the:","choices":["Isthmus","Fundus","Internal os","External os"],"answer":0,"exp":"The isthmus is the constricted region between the body and the cervix. — Slide 34 — Isthmus"},{"q":"In the cervix, the opening to the vagina is called the:","choices":["Internal os","External os","Fornix","Isthmus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The external os is the cervical opening to the vagina; the internal os opens to the isthmus. — Slide 34 — external os: opening to vagina"},{"q":"The three concentric tunics of the uterine wall, from outer to inner, are:","choices":["Endometrium, myometrium, perimetrium","Myometrium, perimetrium, endometrium","Perimetrium, myometrium, endometrium","Serosa, submucosa, mucosa"],"answer":2,"exp":"From outer to inner the uterine wall is the perimetrium, myometrium, and endometrium. — Slide 35, 39 — Uterine layers"},{"q":"Which uterine tunic is the thick middle layer of smooth muscle that becomes distended during pregnancy?","choices":["Perimetrium","Adventitia","Endometrium","Myometrium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The myometrium is the thick middle tunic of smooth muscle (three intertwining layers) that enlarges during pregnancy. — Slide 35, 39 — Myometrium: smooth muscle"},{"q":"In which region of the uterine tube does fertilization of the secondary oocyte usually occur (the long, wide portion)?","choices":["Ampulla","Infundibulum","Isthmus","Intramural (interstitial) portion"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ampulla is the long, wide portion of the uterine tube and the usual site of fertilization. — Slide 45, 51 — Ampulla: long, wide portion"},{"q":"The finger-like projections at the distal end of the uterine tube are the:","choices":["Villi","Fimbriae","Fornices","Cilia"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fimbriae are the finger-like projections at the distal (infundibular) end of the uterine tube. — Slide 51 — Fimbriae: finger-like projections at distal end"},{"q":"The mucosa of the uterine tube is lined by which epithelium?","choices":["Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Simple ciliated columnar epithelium","Transitional epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"The uterine tube is lined by ciliated (and non-ciliated) simple columnar epithelium; ciliary movement helps move the ovum toward the uterus. — Slide 45, 53 — simple ciliated columnar epithelium"},{"q":"Which statement about the relationship of the uterine tube to the ovary is correct?","choices":["The tube is physically fused to the ovary","The tube is continuous with the ovarian cortex","The tube attaches to the ovary via the round ligament","The tube does not physically attach to the ovary"],"answer":3,"exp":"The uterine (fallopian) tube receives the ovulated oocyte and attaches to the uterus, but does not physically attach to the ovary. — Slide 49–50 — Does not physically attach to the ovary"},{"q":"The uterine tube is supported by and located within which structure?","choices":["The broad ligament","The round ligament","The uterosacral ligament","The suspensory ligament of the ovary"],"answer":0,"exp":"The uterine tube lies within and is supported by the broad ligament. — Slide 47, 49 — Within broad ligament / supported by the broad ligament"},{"q":"According to the lecture, the nerve supply to the uterine tube is derived from which plexi?","choices":["The ovarian and uterine plexi","The pelvic splanchnic plexus only","The hypogastric and sacral plexi","The pampiniform plexus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The uterine tube receives its nerve supply from the ovarian and uterine plexi (its blood supply is via the ovarian and uterine arteries). — Slide 48 — Uterine Tube: Nerve supply from ovarian and uterine plexi"},{"q":"What is the positional relationship of the vagina to the urethra and rectum?","choices":["Anterior to the urethra and posterior to the rectum","Lateral to both","Posterior to the urethra and anterior to the rectum","Superior to both"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vagina lies posterior to the urethra and anterior to the rectum. — Slide 57 — Posterior to urethra, anterior to rectum"},{"q":"The recess or pocket surrounding the uterine cervix where it projects into the vagina is called the:","choices":["Infundibulum","Vestibule","Hymen","Fornix"],"answer":3,"exp":"The fornix is the pocket surrounding the uterine cervix (the junction with the cervix of the uterus). — Slide 59, 60 — Fornix: junction/pocket around cervix"},{"q":"The wall of the vagina consists of three tunics. What is its outer tunic?","choices":["Adventitia","Serosa","Perimetrium","Peritoneum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The vaginal wall consists of an inner mucosa, a middle muscularis, and an outer adventitia. — Slide 56 — inner mucosa, middle muscularis, outer adventitia"},{"q":"The external sex organs of the female are collectively called the:","choices":["Perineal region","Vulva (pudendum)","Vaginal vestibule","Vaginal fornix"],"answer":1,"exp":"The external genitalia are collectively called the vulva (also called the pudendum). — Slide 66, 72 — external genitalia collectively called the vulva"},{"q":"The labia majora are homologous to which male structure?","choices":["The penis","The bulbourethral gland","The scrotum","The vas deferens"],"answer":2,"exp":"The labia majora are homologous to the scrotum. — Slide 69, 73 — Labia majora homologous to scrotum"},{"q":"The vestibule is the space enclosed by the labia that contains the openings of which structures?","choices":["The ureters and the urinary bladder","The anus, the rectum, and the anal canal","The cervix, the fornix, and the vaginal canal","The urethra, vagina, and vestibular glands"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vestibule contains the openings of the urethra, the vagina, and the vestibular glands. — Slide 70 — Vestibule contains openings: urethra, vagina, vestibular glands"},{"q":"The greater vestibular gland is homologous to which male gland and performs what function?","choices":["The bulbourethral gland; secretes mucus","The prostate gland; secretes prostatic fluid","The seminal vesicle; secretes fructose-rich fluid","The parotid gland; secretes serous saliva"],"answer":0,"exp":"The greater vestibular gland is homologous to the bulbourethral gland and secretes mucus, drained by a duct into the vestibule. — Slide 78 — Greater Vestibular Gland homologous to bulbourethral gland, secretes mucus"},{"q":"The breast lies between which ribs?","choices":["The 4th and 8th ribs","The 2nd and 6th ribs","The 1st and 4th ribs","The 6th and 10th ribs"],"answer":1,"exp":"The breast lies between the 2nd and 6th ribs, between the sternal edge and the midaxillary line. — Slide 79 — Lies between the 2nd and 6th rib"},{"q":"The breast is described as lying two-thirds superficial to which muscle?","choices":["Serratus anterior","Latissimus dorsi","Pectoral muscle","External oblique"],"answer":2,"exp":"About two-thirds of the breast lies superficial to the pectoral muscle, and one-third superficial to the serratus anterior. — Slide 79 — 2/3 superficial to pectoral muscle"},{"q":"The suspensory ligament of the breast that connects the skin to the breast fascia is also known as:","choices":["The cardinal ligament","The round ligament","The broad ligament","Cooper's ligament"],"answer":3,"exp":"The suspensory ligament of the breast is Cooper's ligament, which connects the skin to the breast fascia. — Slide 81 — suspensory ligament (Cooper's ligament)"},{"q":"Lymphatic drainage of the breast is primarily directed toward which region?","choices":["The axilla","The inguinal nodes","The popliteal nodes","The lumbar nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Breast lymphatic drainage is primarily toward the axilla (and also to the collateral side). — Slide 87 — Lymphatics: drainage towards axilla"},{"q":"The medial quadrant of the breast receives its blood supply in part from perforating branches of which artery?","choices":["The lateral thoracic artery","The internal thoracic artery","The thoracoacromial artery","Subscapular artery of the axilla"],"answer":1,"exp":"The medial quadrant is supplied by perforating branches (2, 3, 4) from the internal thoracic artery and by anterior intercostal branches. — Slide 85 — Medial quadrant: perforating branches from internal thoracic artery"}]};
